on Cory Booker."

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:32.465 align:start size:94% position:6%
Oh, fascinating. I've
interviewed Cory Booker before

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:33.366 line:92%
This is going to be great.

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:34.868 align:start size:94% position:6%
"And here's your booklet
on Elizabeth Warren."

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
Okay, cool.
"And Steve Bullock

00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:38.505 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Michael Bennet
and Marianne Williamson

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:42.175 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Andrew Yang."
And there's 23 books.

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:43.109 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:47.180
-And then, so, you start to,
like, think your way out of it

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.948 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're like,
"Well, do I have to read

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.950 line:92%
the book on each one of the 23?"

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.888
Yes, because it will be the on
that I thought I could skimp o

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:57.590
who will have a breakout moment,
and I'll be like,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:58.425 line:92%
"That was amazing, Denise.

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:01.795 align:start size:94% position:6%
She'll be like,
"My name isn't Denise!"

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:05.465 align:start size:94% position:6%
How do you prepare to not only
understand all 20 of them,

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:09.536 align:start size:94% position:6%
