knock-down, drag-out contest

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:38.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you want
whoever gets to the genera

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:40.954 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be tough and to have
proven themselves

00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:42.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
against really difficult
criticism,

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:45.592
even unfair criticism, because
you know what it's going to be

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:46.526 line:92%
like to run against Trump.

00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:47.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's like running
against a blender.

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.262 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, I do think that --
Absolutely.

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:50.997 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that is a counter-argument
to this idea

00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:52.465 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we all need to be nic
to each other.

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:55.368
It's not like Donald Trump needs
another Democratic candidate

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:59.172 line:92%
to point out a flaw in somebod

00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:00.940 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he might one day
run against,

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.942 align:start size:94% position:6%
because he's very good
at finding flaws,

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:05.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
even things that aren't true
-Yes.

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:09.082
-So he doesn't need any help
from, you know, Cory Booker to

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:11.651
tell him something he can make
fun of Pete Buttigieg about.

00:04:11.651 --> 00:04:13.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, yes,
and, I mean, honestly,

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:15.255 line:92%
if you look at history in term

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:17.323 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the modern
presidential contests,

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:19.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
almost always
when there's a big field

00:04:19.626 --> 00:04:21.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
that has really robust
competition --

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:24.197 align:start size:94% position:6%
not every time,
but almost every time --

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:26.633 align:start size:94% position:6%
the candidate from that part
tends to win.

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:28.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because primaries
that are hard to win

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.870 align:start size:94% position:6%
make you a better candidate.
-Yeah.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:31.704 line:92%
-And all of these candidates -

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:33.439 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, all of the Democrats
are interesting.

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:34.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I think
almost all of them

00:04:34.741 --> 00:04:36.009 align:start size:94% position:6%
would make
really good presidents.

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:37.310 align:start size:94% position:6%
They've all had
interesting careers.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:38.645 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're all --
I've met almost all of them.

00:04:38.645 --> 00:04:40.947 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're all interesting people
They're all competent.

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:43.583
But I don't know which of them
would make the best president.

00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:46.486
And the best way I know to fin
out is to see them battle it out

00:04:46.486 --> 00:04:49.122 align:start size:94% position:6%
